  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(1-x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(1-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(1-x)^{2}}\\cdot (1-x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(1-2x+{x}^{2})}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
